<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nimrud</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nimrud, ancient Kalhu, was a city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> that served as a royal capital of the Neo-Assyrian period. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashurnasirpal-ii">Ashurnasirpal II</a> made it his capital in the ninth century BCE and built palaces decorated with stone reliefs. Its royal complexes were funded by the tribute and resources channelled into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire</a>'s heartland.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nimrud.jpg" alt="Nimrud" width="320" height="206" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: M.chohan · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Iraq;_Nimrud_-_Assyria,_Lamassu%27s_Guarding_Palace_Entrance.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Nimrud lies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> about 30 kilometres south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a>, in the Nineveh Plains of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-mesopotamia">Upper Mesopotamia</a>, some 10 kilometres north of the meeting of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a> and its tributary the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-zab">Great Zab</a>. The city covered about 360 hectares. Its Assyrian name was Kalhu, and its biblical name was Calah. The name Nimrud was recorded by Carsten Niebuhr in the mid-18th century, and it spread widely when 19th-century scholars linked the site with Nimrod of Genesis.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Calah stood on a commercial route between Ashur and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nineveh">Nineveh</a>. It began as a trading settlement founded under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shalmaneser-i">Shalmaneser I</a>, but the site had fallen into disrepair when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashurnasirpal-ii">Ashurnasirpal II</a> chose it as his capital. He cleared the ruins and built an entirely new city, with walls 7.5 kilometres long, temples, royal offices, a palace meant to surpass all earlier ones, botanical gardens of foreign plants and a zoo. His inscriptions give no reason for the move; scholars suggest that Ashur had little room left, that Calah was more central, or that he sought independence from Ashur's great families. A stele describes a ten-day celebration in 864 BCE for 69,574 guests.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shalmaneser-iii">Shalmaneser III</a> built a palace of more than 200 rooms and the Great Ziggurat. Tiglath-Pileser III carried out major building and introduced Eastern Aramaic as the empire's common language. Calah was the imperial capital from 879 to 706 BCE, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sargon-ii">Sargon II</a> moved the government to Dur-Sharrukin; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sennacherib">Sennacherib</a> later moved it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nineveh">Nineveh</a>. Assyrian kings were buried at Ashur, but their queens lay at Calah. The city remained a major centre and royal residence until it was largely destroyed during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medo-babylonian-conquest-of-the-assyrian-empire">Medo-Babylonian conquest of the Assyrian Empire</a>, between 626 and 609 BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austen-henry-layard">Austen Henry Layard</a> began excavating Nimrud in 1845, and he shipped two colossal winged guardian figures to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a> in 1847. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/max-mallowan">Max Mallowan</a>'s team resumed work in 1949 and found the 244 Nimrud Letters. In 1988 the Iraqi Department of Antiquities found four queens' tombs. The excavations revealed bas-reliefs, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivories</a>, a well-preserved statue of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashurnasirpal-ii">Ashurnasirpal II</a> and the palaces of Ashurnasirpal II, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shalmaneser-iii">Shalmaneser III</a> and Tiglath-Pileser III. Objects from the site are held in at least 76 museums worldwide. In 2015 much of the excavated remains were destroyed, and reconstruction work has followed.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nimrud” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>At its peak the Neo-Assyrian economy channelled the agricultural surpluses of Mesopotamia, the timber of Lebanon, the metals of Anatolia, the horses of the Zagros and the luxury goods of Phoenician trade into the construction of enormous royal cities at <b>Nimrud</b>, Khorsabad and Nineveh.</blockquote><a href="/empire/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Carved ivory panels for furniture in Phoenician styles have been found in the Assyrian palaces at <b>Nimrud</b> and at Samaria, capital of the Kingdom of Israel, where the Bible speaks of Ahab’s “ivory house”.</blockquote><a href="/culture/phoenicians">Phoenicians · Arts &amp; Knowledge</a></li></ul></section>
